{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And HaShem hardened, etc., and he refused, etc.</b> The verse testifies that this time he firmly resolved in his mind to negate the mission of Israel, and nothing remained in his mind to think thoughts about whether he would feel the plagues or whether he would suffer great plagues, but rather he sealed the matter: let the word of HaShem be transgressed even though he be killed." "<b>Or</b> it may mean that he cleverly devised a plan so that plagues would no longer come upon him, and in this there would be no need to send them away. And this is what he said immediately after saying ״and he refused״: <b>And Pharaoh said to him, Get you away from me</b>, etc. The intention in this is that since he understood from the nine plagues that came upon him — all of which followed this pattern: two with warning and one without warning; according to the one who says there were three weeks of warning for each and every plague, and according to the one who says one week — he said: from now on, in this coming plague, a warning must precede, and if there is no warning there is no plague. Therefore he cleverly devised and said: ״Take heed to yourself, see my face no more״ — and since this is so, there is no warning, and since there is no warning there is no plague. Now HaShem did not harden his heart to do this, except after the nine plagues were exhausted and only the plague of the firstborn remained. And therefore Moses said: <b>As you have spoken, I will see your face again no more</b>, etc. And the explanation of Moses' words I have already explained in parashat Va'era in the verse (Exodus 7:3) ״And I will harden, and Pharaoh will not hearken to you״, etc." "<b>And by way of</b> remez (allusion), it may intend in his saying <b>As you have spoken</b> a response to what he said to him: ״On the day you see my face you shall die״ — he answered him that he cannot kill, and all that is in his hand is only the power of speech, and that is his saying ״As you have spoken״ and not ״As you shall do.״ Or it may intend on this manner: you have already spoken thus once before, and the point is — see what has come of your efforts, as they said, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 1:31), on the verse (Exodus 2:15) ״And he sought to kill Moses״ — and it did not succeed in his hand; they also said (Yalkut Shimoni 175) that he killed all his guards who had let Moses enter, and they answered that they had no authority over him. If so, given the contempt — how does he presume to say ״On the day, etc., you shall die״? And corresponding to the matter itself — that you do not wish to see my face, I too do not wish to look upon your form — ״I will see your face again no more״, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {2 "The citation ילקוט שמעוני קע״ה uses the alphabetic-numerical reference system of Yalkut Shimoni on Exodus; the specific remez number is rendered as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:40.908284101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}